first in terraform instance update all 8 services in terraform.tfvars in ecr repository configuration

now in deployment instance clone the code i.e. microservices. install helm docker eksctl kubectl

go to ecr service and by using push command push the docker immage to respected repositories

then create helm folder in microservicesdemo folder, before creating yaml file create namespaces. create the yaml files here only



creating namespace command:
        kubectl apply -f <namespace name.yaml>



commands after finishing the yaml files: 

Once these files are saved in your workspace, you can install or upgrade all 8 microservices with a single command from your project root:
		helm upgrade --install crm-app ./microservice  ----- user should be in location /root/microservicedemo/helm

Check the Pods:
Once the Helm command succeeds, it will deploy all 8 microservices across their respective namespaces in one shot! You can watch them all spin up at once with this command:
		kubectl get pods -A | grep -E 'auth|gateway|user|admin|employee|customer|hr|task'


Check the Services:
		kubectl get svc -A | grep -E 'auth|gateway|user|admin|employee|customer|hr|task'
		
Verify the new Gateway pod is ready
		kubectl get pods -n gateway-ns
		
		
Check the Kubernetes Events:
		kubectl describe pod -l app=admin-service -n admin-ns
		kubectl describe pod -l app=gateway-service -n gateway-ns | grep -A 10 "Environment:"
		
Check the Application Logs:
		kubectl logs -l app=admin-service -n admin-ns
		kubectl logs -l app=auth-service -n auth-ns
		kubectl logs -l app=gateway-service -n gateway-ns --tail=50
		
Get Your Public URL (The External Entryway)
		kubectl get svc gateway-service -n gateway-ns


Run a simple Health Check (The Safest Test)
  Before guessing your specific API routes, let's ask the Gateway if it is healthy.
  Run this curl command right from your Linux terminal (replace the URL with your exact AWS link):
		curl -X GET http://ad2790679807b48719913730a48f9173-499939766.us-east-1.elb.amazonaws.com:8080/actuator/health
		
		
		
Test a Routed Microservice (e.g., Auth Service)
  The entire job of an API Gateway is to take external requests and route them to your internal ClusterIP services (like auth-service or user-service).
  Depending on how your developers wrote the routing rules in the Gateway code, you usually just append the service name or route path to the end of the URL.

If you have an Auth Login endpoint:
		curl -X POST http://ad2790679807b48719913730a48f9173-499939766.us-east-1.elb.amazonaws.com:8080/api/auth/login \
		-H "Content-Type: application/json" \
		-d '{"username": "dbadmin", "password": "dbpassword123"}'
		
		
		
		
Creating Secrets:
Create a secret from literal values (username/password):
		kubectl create secret generic my-secret \
		  --from-literal=username=admin \
		  --from-literal=password=secret123 \
          -n <my-namespace like gateway-ns>


Viewing & Inspecting Secrets:
List all secrets in a namespace:
		kubectl get secrets -n <my-namespace>
		
Describe a secret (Shows keys stored inside, but not the actual secret values):
		kubectl describe secret my-secret -n <my-namespace>
		
To view all secrets across every single namespace in your cluster at once:
		kubectl get secrets --all-namespaces